WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.585
 ♪  

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:04.796
 [Ricko] We have to hunt 
 and kill to survive. 

00:00:04.796 --> 00:00:06.423
 Just like the animals out here. 

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:13.430
 ♪  

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:20.437
 ♪  

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:26.317
 ♪  

00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:34.534
 ♪  

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:37.037
 Most likely the wolves came 
 along and hamstringed it, 

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:40.832
 or they're right around here 
 somewhere. 

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:42.459
 I'm traveling along 
 with my snow machine, 

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.419
 looking for a place 
 to do some wolf snares, 

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:48.048
 and I come upon a dead, 
 looks like a calf moose. 

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.382
 From what I can see, 
 there's some blood. 

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:51.843
 It might have been bit around 
 the stomach or the back leg. 

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:53.470
 And there's a lot 
 of snow right now, 

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.263
 the moose are real vulnerable. 

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:57.307
 And, um, I don't think 
 they could survive much 

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.726
 if they're attacked by a wolf 
 or a pack of wolves. 

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:02.729
 So I'm guessing what I'm seeing 
 right here is a, uh, place 

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.856
 where moose are getting 
 chewed up by a wolf 

00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:06.191
 and just kind of died. 

00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:07.901
 If I find something like this, 
 it's unfortunate. 

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.444
 I don't like to see this 
 happen to a moose, 

00:01:09.444 --> 00:01:11.237
 but it's part of the cycle 
 of life out here, 

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:14.407
 but the wolves will be back, 
 though, for sure. 

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.202
 The wolves are most likely 
 right around here is my guess, 

00:01:17.202 --> 00:01:18.912
 and they'll be back tonight. 

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:20.330
 This is part of nature out here. 

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.540
 There were calves, the weakest 
 ones get killed a lot, 

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:23.708
 especially in deep snow. 

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:27.170
 I mean, you look at the snow 
 out here, if you walk out here, 

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.756
 just right off the trail, 
 it's this deep. 

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.508
 And then you go into where 
 the willows are hung, 

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:33.927
 it's, it's this deep out here. 

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.137
 And that, that calf 
 really has no chance 

00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:39.390
 against predators out here 
 and, um, it's unfortunate. 

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:40.391
 This time of the year, 

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:42.644
 the, the numbers of moose 
 get knocked down 

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:44.771
 and that's what, uh, 
 predators are doing out here 

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:47.107
 is hunting moose. 

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:48.942
 So, I'll keep moving. 

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.568
 I'll just leave it 
 the way it is 

00:01:50.568 --> 00:01:52.570
 and start looking around 
 for wolf tracks. 

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:58.409
 ♪  

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:00.829
 ♪  

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:02.872
 When it comes to food 
 with us Athabascans, 

00:02:02.872 --> 00:02:05.083
 moose is number one staple 
 of our diet. 

00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.544
 And that's number one staple 
 of the wolf, too. 

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:09.629
 You're talking 
 between six wolves 

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.297
 and 15 wolves in one area. 

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.341
 That's about 
 the standard size pack. 

00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:15.009
 And when you have 
 a standard size pack like this, 

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:16.719
 they kill about a moose a week. 

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:18.012
 So you do the math on that, 

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:21.474
 that's a lot of moose they kill 
 over a set period of time. 

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.144
 When you have a pack of wolves 
 in your area, or multiple packs, 

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.354
 you lose a lot of moose 
 population around your area. 

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:29.691
 So traditionally, we really have 
 a big part of our culture 

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:31.776
 as wolfing or predator control. 

00:02:31.776 --> 00:02:34.487
 Especially in the winter when 
 the moose are most vulnerable. 

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:37.115
 It's kind of like how humans 
 have cattle in certain areas. 

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:38.825
 They have to take care 
 of their cattle. 

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:41.035
 With us, moose is our cattle. 

00:02:41.035 --> 00:02:42.745
 So we have to look out 
 for that animal. 

00:02:42.745 --> 00:02:46.958
 We can't just hunt moose and not 
 look out for the moose itself. 

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:48.042
 I got one snare up here. 

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:49.836
 I'll check it out. 

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:54.174
 See some fresh tracks going in, 
 so might be promising. 

00:02:55.842 --> 00:03:01.764
 ♪  

00:03:01.764 --> 00:03:03.183
 ♪  

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:06.644
 Tracks going back here. 

00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:10.356
 Put the snare 
 kind of far back here. 

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:11.524
 Oh. 

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:14.903
 Trying to punch through. 

00:03:14.903 --> 00:03:16.529
 A big old trench. 

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.909
 See where they went 
 through the top here? 

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:25.872
 ♪  

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:29.250
 Oh. 

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.418
 Holy, I got one. 

00:03:32.128 --> 00:03:33.504
 [Ricko] Holy. 

00:03:33.504 --> 00:03:37.008
 Anaa baasee', Dinaahuto. 

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:37.884
 Beautiful wolf. 

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.302
 I don't want 
 to rip it away too hard 

00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:42.263
 because that's frozen down. 

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:43.973
 That's a good-sized wolf. 

00:03:43.973 --> 00:03:49.896
 ♪  

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.148
 Anaa baasee', teekkona. 

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:54.609
 Teekkona is wolf. 

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:56.778
 I got to get this out of here. 

00:03:56.778 --> 00:04:00.490
 ♪  

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:01.658
 When I put out wolf sets, 

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:03.785
 I don't always expect 
 to catch something. 

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:06.120
 And once you're able 
 to have that luck 

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.747
 where the animal come to you, 

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.667
 you have to really show your 
 appreciation for that animal 

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:12.126
 by saying thank you. 

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:16.172
 Because I know how much 
 it means to my people. 

00:04:16.172 --> 00:04:18.633
 There's only a few of us 
 out here that can do that. 

00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:20.927
 I feel proud to say 
 I'm one of them. 

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:23.930
 Blessed to have that wolf 
 bring itself to me this way. 

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.642
 I really, truly believe that. 

00:04:27.642 --> 00:04:29.686
 Let's keep moving, 
 keep checking sets, 

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:32.939
 and when I'm done, 
 I'll start skinning. 

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.941
 I got a lot of daylight, 
 it's warm out. 

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.111
 Anaa baasee'. 

